---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../lib/tool-metadata';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Common JSON Errors and How to Fix Them',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'JSONCraft' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'JSONCraft' },
    datePublished: '2025-01-01',
    dateModified: '2025-01-01',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE.url },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Guides', item: SITE.url + '/guides' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Common JSON Errors', item: SITE.url + '/guides/common-json-errors' },
    ],
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Common JSON Errors and How to Fix Them | JSONCraft" description="Identify and resolve the most frequent JSON syntax errors developers encounter. Practical fixes with examples." canonical={SITE.url + '/guides/common-json-errors'} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Header />
  <main>
    <div class="container content-section">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <a href="/guides">Guides</a>
        <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <span class="cur" aria-current="page">Common JSON Errors</span>
      </nav>

      <h1>Common JSON Errors and How to Fix Them</h1>
      <p class="section-text">JSON is simple by design, but its strict syntax catches many developers off guard. This guide covers the most frequent errors and exactly how to fix them.</p>

      <h2>1. Trailing Comma</h2>
      <p class="section-text">The most common JSON error. Unlike JavaScript object literals, JSON forbids trailing commas after the last element.</p>
      <h3>Wrong</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Alice&quot;,&#10;  &quot;age&quot;: 30,&#10;}' /></pre>
      <h3>Correct</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Alice&quot;,&#10;  &quot;age&quot;: 30&#10;}' /></pre>
      <p class="section-text"><strong>Fix:</strong> Remove the comma after the last property.</p>

      <h2>2. Comments in JSON</h2>
      <p class="section-text">JSON does not support comments of any kind. No //, no /* */, no #.</p>
      <h3>Wrong</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Alice&quot;, // This is a comment&#10;  &quot;age&quot;: 30&#10;}' /></pre>
      <h3>Correct</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Alice&quot;,&#10;  &quot;age&quot;: 30&#10;}' /></pre>
      <p class="section-text"><strong>Fix:</strong> Remove all comments. Use a separate documentation file or a "_comment" key if needed.</p>

      <h2>3. Single Quotes</h2>
      <p class="section-text">JSON strings must use double quotes. Single quotes cause a parse error.</p>
      <h3>Wrong</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  \'name\': \'Alice\'&#10;}' /></pre>
      <h3>Correct</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Alice&quot;&#10;}' /></pre>
      <p class="section-text"><strong>Fix:</strong> Replace all single quotes with double quotes.</p>

      <h2>4. Missing Commas Between Properties</h2>
      <p class="section-text">Every property in an object (except the last) must be separated by a comma.</p>
      <h3>Wrong</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Alice&quot;&#10;  &quot;age&quot;: 30&#10;}' /></pre>
      <h3>Correct</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Alice&quot;,&#10;  &quot;age&quot;: 30&#10;}' /></pre>
      <p class="section-text"><strong>Fix:</strong> Add commas between properties.</p>

      <h2>5. Unquoted Keys</h2>
      <p class="section-text">Object property names must be double-quoted strings in JSON.</p>
      <h3>Wrong</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  name: &quot;Alice&quot;&#10;}' /></pre>
      <h3>Correct</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Alice&quot;&#10;}' /></pre>
      <p class="section-text"><strong>Fix:</strong> Wrap all keys in double quotes.</p>

      <h2>6. Undefined Values</h2>
      <p class="section-text">JavaScript undefined is not a valid JSON value. Use null instead.</p>
      <h3>Wrong</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Alice&quot;,&#10;  &quot;nickname&quot;: undefined&#10;}' /></pre>
      <h3>Correct</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Alice&quot;,&#10;  &quot;nickname&quot;: null&#10;}' /></pre>
      <p class="section-text"><strong>Fix:</strong> Replace undefined with null.</p>

      <h2>7. Numbers with Leading Zeros</h2>
      <p class="section-text">Numbers cannot have leading zeros (except for 0 itself and 0.x decimal values).</p>
      <h3>Wrong</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;code&quot;: 007&#10;}' /></pre>
      <h3>Correct</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;code&quot;: 7&#10;}' /></pre>
      <p class="section-text"><strong>Fix:</strong> Remove leading zeros from numbers.</p>

      <h2>8. Unescaped Characters in Strings</h2>
      <p class="section-text">Certain characters must be escaped in JSON strings: double quotes, backslashes, and control characters.</p>
      <h3>Wrong</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&quot;path&quot;: &quot;C:\\Users\\Alice&quot;}' /></pre>
      <h3>Correct</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&quot;path&quot;: &quot;C:\\\\Users\\\\Alice&quot;}' /></pre>
      <p class="section-text"><strong>Fix:</strong> Escape special characters with a backslash.</p>

      <h2>9. BOM (Byte Order Mark)</h2>
      <p class="section-text">Some text editors add an invisible BOM character at the start of files. This causes JSON parse errors.</p>
      <p class="section-text"><strong>Fix:</strong> Save the file without BOM. In most editors, this is a save option (UTF-8 without BOM).</p>

      <h2>10. Using the Wrong Data Type</h2>
      <p class="section-text">JSON has specific types: string, number, boolean, null, array, and object. Using other JavaScript types causes errors.</p>
      <h3>Wrong</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;created&quot;: new Date(),&#10;  &quot;regex&quot;: /pattern/&#10;}' /></pre>
      <h3>Correct</h3>
      <pre><code set:html='{&#10;  &quot;created&quot;: &quot;2025-01-15T10:30:00Z&quot;,&#10;  &quot;regex&quot;: &quot;pattern&quot;&#10;}' /></pre>
      <p class="section-text"><strong>Fix:</strong> Convert non-JSON types to strings or numbers before serializing.</p>

      <h2>How to Catch These Errors</h2>
      <p class="section-text">Use the <a href="/json-validator">JSON Validator</a> to check your JSON before using it. It provides clear error messages that point to the exact location of the problem.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Paste your JSON into the validator.</li>
        <li>If it is invalid, read the error message carefully.</li>
        <li>The error position usually indicates which line and which character caused the issue.</li>
        <li>Fix that specific issue and validate again.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="privacy-note" style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
        <span class="icon">&#128274;</span>
        <div>Validate your JSON instantly with the <a href="/json-validator">JSON Validator</a>. All processing happens in your browser.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
